  "theory": {
    "intro": "Differentiating $e^{f(x)}$ by the chain rule gives $f'(x)e^{f(x)}$, so integrating reverses that: $\\int f'(x)e^{f(x)}\\,dx = e^{f(x)} + C$. The inner derivative $f'(x)$ must already appear as a factor, up to a constant multiple.",
    "facts": [
      "The **inner function** $f(x)$ is the index of $e$.",
      "$\\int f'(x)e^{f(x)}\\,dx = e^{f(x)} + C$: the index is unchanged and the factor disappears.",
      "The factor $f'(x)$ must be present, so $\\int e^{x^{2}}\\,dx$ cannot be found by this method.",
      "A constant multiple of $f'(x)$ is enough: divide by that constant at the end.",
      "Constants can be moved across the integral sign, but variable factors cannot.",
      "In $\\int 2xe^{x^{2}+5}\\,dx$ the inner function is $x^{2}+5$ and $f'(x) = 2x$ is already there.",
      "$\\int xe^{x^{2}}\\,dx = \\dfrac{1}{2}e^{x^{2}} + C$, because $x$ is half of $f'(x) = 2x$.",
      "A negative inner derivative makes the adjusting constant negative, as in $\\int xe^{-x^{2}}\\,dx = -\\dfrac{1}{2}e^{-x^{2}} + C$.",
      "A definite integral is evaluated as $\\left[e^{f(x)}\\right]_{p}^{q}$, with the limits unchanged.",
      "Check any primitive by differentiating it with the chain rule."
    ],
    "steps": [
      "Identify the inner function $f(x)$, the index of $e$.",
      "Check that $f'(x)$ appears as a factor, up to a constant multiple.",
      "Adjust for any missing constant factor.",
      "Write the result as $e^{f(x)} + C$."
    ]
  },
